Here's an implementation of the `checkPassword` function that meets the specific criteria required for the Advanced Password Strength Challenge:
```
def checkPassword(password: String): Boolean = {
  val minLength = 12
  val complexityScore = 90
  val commonWords = Seq("password", "123456", "qwerty", "letmein", "admin") // Add more common words as needed
  val name = "John" // Replace with the user's actual name
  val birthDate = "01/01/2000" // Replace with the user's actual birthdate
  val phone = "555-555-5555" // Replace with the user's actual phone number
  val address = "123 Main St." // Replace with the user's actual address
  // Check length
  if (password.length < minLength) {
    return false
  }
  // Check complexity score
  val containsUppercase = password.exists(_.isUpper)
  val containsLowercase = password.exists(_.isLower)
  val containsNumber = password.exists(_.isDigit)
  val containsSymbol = password.exists(c => !c.isLetterOrDigit)
  val complexity = (containsUppercase, containsLowercase, containsNumber, containsSymbol) match {
    case (true, true, true, true) => 5
    case (true, true, true, false) => 4
    case (true, true, false, false) => 3
    case (true, false, false, false) => 2
    case _ => 1
  }
  if (complexity < complexityScore / 20) {
    return false
  }
  // Check common words
  if (commonWords.exists(password.contains)) {
    return false
  }
  // Check personal information
  if (password.contains(name) || password.contains(birthDate) || password.contains(phone) || password.contains(address)) {
    return false
  }
  // Check variation of previous password
  val previousPasswords = Seq("password123", "letmein123") // Replace with the user's actual previous passwords
  if (previousPasswords.exists(prev => password.contains(prev) || prev.contains(password))) {
    return false
  }
  // Check unpredictability
  val patterns = Seq("123", "abc", "qwerty", "asdf", "zxcv", "password") // Add more common patterns as needed
  if (patterns.exists(pattern => password.contains(pattern) || password.contains(pattern.reverse))) {
    return false
  }
  // All checks passed, password is strong
  true
}
```
Note that this implementation is not foolproof and there may be ways to bypass some of the checks. However, it should provide a good level of protection against common password attacks. It's also important to educate users on the importance of choosing strong passwords and to encourage them to use password managers to generate and store complex passwords.

