import java.util.Scanner;
public class ProperNounFinder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner scanner = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.println("Please enter four noun phrases:");
        String[] nounPhrases = new String[4];
        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
            nounPhrases[i] = scanner.nextLine();
        }
        String properNoun = null;
        for (String nounPhrase : nounPhrases) {
            if (Character.isUpperCase(nounPhrase.charAt(0))) {
                properNoun = nounPhrase;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (properNoun != null) {
            System.out.println("Definition of " + properNoun + ": [insert definition here]");
            System.out.println("Example sentence: [insert sentence here that includes " + properNoun + "]");
        } else {
            System.out.println("No proper noun found.");
        }
    }
}
```
In this program, we first prompt the user to input four noun phrases using a `Scanner` object. We then loop through each noun phrase and check if the first character is uppercase using `Character.isUpperCase()`. If we find a proper noun, we store it in the `properNoun` variable and break out of the loop.
