What is the proper XML structure for creating a multimedia player in JavaScript that supports audio, video, and interactive content? Can you provide a code example for each type of multimedia and explain the differences between them in terms of supported file formats and playback options? 
[XML data]
<player>
  <audio src="audio.mp3"></audio>
  <video src="video.mp4"></video>
  <interactive>
    <iframe src="interactive.html"></iframe>
  </interactive>
</player>
[XML code]
<script>
  const audioPlayer = document.querySelector('player audio');
  const videoPlayer = document.querySelector('player video');
  const interactivePlayer = document.querySelector('player interactive iframe');
  // Audio Player
  audioPlayer.controls = true;
  audioPlayer.autoplay = false;
  audioPlayer.preload = 'metadata';
  // Video Player
  videoPlayer.controls = true;
  videoPlayer.autoplay = false;
  videoPlayer.preload = 'metadata';
  // Interactive Player
  interactivePlayer.style.width = '100%';
  interactivePlayer.style.height = '100%';
</script>
Please include relevant use cases for each type of multimedia, such as audio for background music, video for instructional content, and interactive content for quizzes or games. Format your answer using Latex.

output:
The proper XML structure for creating a multimedia player in JavaScript that supports audio, video, and interactive content is as follows:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=XML]
<player>
  <audio src="audio.mp3"></audio>
  <video src="video.mp4"></video>
  <interactive>
    <iframe src="interactive.html"></iframe>
  </interactive>
</player>
\end{lstlisting}
This XML code creates a multimedia player that supports three types of content: audio, video, and interactive. The audio and video elements have a "src" attribute that specifies the location of the audio and video files. The interactive element contains an iframe that points to an HTML file that contains the interactive content.
To create the multimedia player in JavaScript, we can use the following code:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=JavaScript]
const audioPlayer = document.querySelector('player audio');
const videoPlayer = document.querySelector('player video');
const interactivePlayer = document.querySelector('player interactive iframe');
// Audio Player
audioPlayer.controls = true;
audioPlayer.autoplay = false;
audioPlayer.preload = 'metadata';
// Video Player
videoPlayer.controls = true;
videoPlayer.autoplay = false;
videoPlayer.preload = 'metadata';
// Interactive Player
interactivePlayer.style.width = '100%';
interactivePlayer.style.height = '100%';
\end{lstlisting}
This JavaScript code selects the audio, video, and interactive elements from the XML code and sets their properties to create a multimedia player. The "controls" property adds playback controls to the player, "autoplay" specifies whether the player should start playing automatically, and "preload" specifies whether the player should load the entire file or just the metadata.
In terms of supported file formats and playback options, audio files can be in MP3, WAV, or OGG format, and can be played back with controls such as play, pause, and volume. Video files can be in MP4, AVI, or WMV format, and can be played back with controls such as play, pause, volume, and full screen. Interactive content can be in HTML format and can include quizzes, games, and other interactive elements.
Some use cases for each type of multimedia include:
- Audio: background music for a website or podcast
- Video: instructional content for a tutorial or online course
- Interactive content: quizzes or games to engage users and test their knowledge.

