To calculate the gravitational force between two objects in JavaScript, you can use the formula:
F = (G * m1 * m2) / d^2
Where F is the gravitational force, G is the gravitational constant (6.67 x 10^-11 Nm^2/kg^2), m1 and m2 are the masses of the two objects, and d is the distance between them.
To calculate the force needed to keep a 2-kilogram object from being pulled towards two other objects weighing 10 and 5 kilograms, respectively, you can calculate the gravitational force between the 2-kilogram object and each of the other objects, and then add the two forces together. The force needed to keep the 2-kilogram object in place would be equal and opposite to this combined gravitational force.
Here is the JavaScript code to perform these calculations:
```javascript
const G = 6.67e-11; // gravitational constant
// function to calculate gravitational force between two objects
function calcGravForce(m1, m2, d) {
  const force = (G * m1 * m2) / (d * d);
  return force;
}
// masses of the objects in kg
const m1 = 10;
const m2 = 5;
const m3 = 2;
// distances between the objects in meters
const d1 = 2;
const d2 = 3;
// calculate gravitational forces between the objects
const f1 = calcGravForce(m1, m3, d1);
const f2 = calcGravForce(m2, m3, d2);
// calculate the total force needed to keep the 2-kg object in place
const totalForce = f1 + f2;
console.log("Total force needed:", totalForce.toFixed(2), "N");
```
In this example, the gravitational force between the 2-kg object and the 10-kg object is calculated using a distance of 2 meters, and the gravitational force between the 2-kg object and the 5-kg object is calculated using a distance of 3 meters. The two forces are then added together to get the total force needed to keep the 2-kg object in place.

