Thank you for the task. To solve this problem, we will use the Kalman filter algorithm. The Kalman filter is a recursive algorithm that estimates the state of a system with noisy measurements. It uses a mathematical model of the system and statistical information about the noise to predict the state of the system and update the estimate based on the new measurements.
Here is a MATLAB function that implements the Kalman filter algorithm for the given system:
```
function [x_hat, K] = kalman_filter(A, B, C, Q, R, x0, y)
% Initialization
n = size(A, 1); % Number of states
m = size(B, 2); % Number of inputs
T = length(y); % Number of time steps
x_hat = zeros(n, T); % Estimated state vector
x_hat(:, 1) = x0; % Initial state estimate
P = eye(n); % Initial error covariance matrix
K = zeros(n, m, T); % Kalman gain matrix
% Kalman filter algorithm
for t = 2:T
    % Prediction step
    x_hat(:, t) = A*x_hat(:, t-1) + B*u(:, t-1);
    P = A*P*A' + Q;

    % Update step
    K(:,:,t) = P*C'/(C*P*C' + R);
    x_hat(:, t) = x_hat(:, t) + K(:,:,t)*(y(:, t) - C*x_hat(:, t));
    P = (eye(n) - K(:,:,t)*C)*P;
end
end
```
To tune the filter parameters, we can use the following steps:
1. Choose initial values for Q and R.
2. Run the Kalman filter algorithm with these values and compute the mean squared error between the estimated state vector and the true state vector.
3. Adjust the values of Q and R and repeat step 2 until the mean squared error is minimized.
Here is an example of how to use the function to estimate the state of the system:
```
% Define the system matrices
A = [1 1; 0 1];
B = [0.5; 1];
C = [1 0];
Q = [0.1 0; 0 0.1];
R = 1;
x0 = [0; 0];
% Generate sample measurement data
u = randn(1, 100);
w = sqrt(Q)*randn(2, 100);
v = sqrt(R)*randn(1, 100);
x = zeros(2, 100);
y = zeros(1, 100);
for k = 2:100
    x(:, k) = A*x(:, k-1) + B*u(k-1) + w(:, k);
    y(:, k) = C*x(:, k) + v(:, k);
end
% Run the Kalman filter algorithm
[x_hat, K] = kalman_filter(A, B, C, Q, R, x0, y);
% Compute the mean squared error
mse = mean((x - x_hat).^2, 'all');
disp(['Mean squared error: ', num2str(mse)])
```
In this example, we generate sample measurement data using random noise and run the Kalman filter algorithm to estimate the state of the system. We then compute the mean squared error between the estimated state vector and the true state vector. We can adjust the values of Q and R to minimize this error.

