To sort a vector of structures based on a particular field in ascending order, you can use the `sort` function from the `<algorithm>` library in C++. 
Here is an example implementation of a function that accepts a vector of Person structures and a string indicating the field to be sorted, and returns the sorted vector:
```
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
struct Person {
  std::string name;
  int age;
};
bool compareByName(const Person& a, const Person& b) {
  return a.name < b.name;
}
bool compareByAge(const Person& a, const Person& b) {
  return a.age < b.age;
}
std::vector<Person> sortPeopleByField(std::vector<Person> people, std::string field) {
  if (field == "name") {
    std::sort(people.begin(), people.end(), compareByName);
  } else if (field == "age") {
    std::sort(people.begin(), people.end(), compareByAge);
  }
  return people;
}
```
In this implementation, we first define the `Person` structure and two comparison functions: `compareByName` and `compareByAge`. These functions take two `Person` structures and return a boolean indicating whether the first argument should come before the second argument in the sorted vector. 
The `sortPeopleByField` function takes a vector of `Person` structures and a string indicating the field to be sorted. If the field is "name", we call `std::sort` with the `compareByName` function as the third argument. If the field is "age", we call `std::sort` with the `compareByAge` function as the third argument. Finally, we return the sorted vector.

